{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["And the reason for <b>but you, if you have gone astray</b> is connected with \"may YHWH make you.\" He means: but you, as you have gone astray in defilement under your husband, and as you have become defiled and a man other than your husband has lain with you — may YHWH make you a curse and an oath. But because of the length of the conditions, he returned to say a second time \"and the priest shall adjure the woman,\" and to explain that it is an oath of curse. And Ibn Ezra explained that the first \"and he shall adjure\" is in the Name and the second in the curse, as is explicitly stated. But this is not correct, for with the Name one does not adjure in the language of \"if.\" And behold, there is nothing in all the laws of the Torah dependent on a miracle except this matter, which is a fixed wonder and miracle performed in Israel when the majority of them do the will of the Place, for He desired, for the sake of His righteousness, to discipline the women so that they not commit the excesses of immorality of the nations, and to purify Israel from mamzerut so that they would be worthy to cause the Shekhinah to dwell among them. And therefore this matter ceased from the time they became corrupted through transgressions, as they said (Sotah 47a): \"From the time adulterers multiplied, the waters of the sotah ceased,\" as it is stated (Hosea 4:14): \"I will not punish your daughters when they commit harlotry, nor your daughters-in-law when they commit adultery, for they themselves consort with harlots and sacrifice with cult-prostitutes, and a people without understanding shall be beaten.\" And Scripture does not say that the adulterous women are exempt from the sin because their husbands are adulterers, but only that this great miracle, which is performed for their honor and for their being a holy people, will not be performed for them — and they will not understand this benefit nor desire it. And this is what it says, \"and a people without understanding shall be beaten (ילבט),\" meaning, it will be caught in its own folly. And likewise, \"and the fool of lips shall be beaten (ילבט)\" (Proverbs 10:8) — the Jerusalem Targum translates: \"and the fool with his lips is joined together (מתאחד).\" And this is the reason for what they said (Sotah 47a): \"'And the man shall be cleared of iniquity' — at a time when the man is cleared of iniquity, the waters examine his wife; if the man is not cleared of iniquity, the waters do not examine his wife.\" And the clearance is that he had not had relations with her from the time he warned her and she secluded herself. And some explain that if the husband committed any forbidden act of intercourse all his days, the waters do not examine her. And what emerges from the halakhah (there) is that even if his sons and daughters were adulterers and he was not careless about them, they would not examine. And the general principle is that it is a miracle and an honor for Israel:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The phrase המתרגם הירושלמי refers to the Jerusalem Targum (Targum Yerushalmi / Pseudo-Jonathan) on Proverbs 10:8; Ramban cites its Aramaic rendering of ילבט as מתאחד, literally 'is joined / united,' understood here in the sense of being ensnared in folly. The word מקום ('the Place') is a rabbinic epithet for God, here transliterated rather than translated in accordance with the voice rule that the names of God are never translated into English; however, since מקום is not a proper divine name but a descriptive epithet, the translation retains it as 'the Place,' which is the standard English rendering in rabbinic literature."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:15.900836564Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}